soft-currency economy loop (earn via enemy\\n     defeat, auto-spend at a threshold for a temporary buff, buff decays)\\n     layered on single-player -- a genuine common F2P building block, not a\\n     claim that F2P is itself a mechanic\" \"indie -- the smallest possible starter kernel in the repo (one entity,\\n     one movement system, nothing else) -- a genuinely useful rapid-\\n     iteration starting point, explicitly labelled as a scaffold not a\\n     genre mechanic\" \"post-apocalyptic -- a real resource-scarcity system (limited weapon\\n     defatom 'charges' depleting per use, replenished by scarce 'salvage'\\n     pickups) layered on single-player -- a genuine scarcity mechanic, not\\n     a claim that the narrative setting is inherently a distinct core loop\"], :verification \"every author.clj actually run via `clojure -M author.clj`, confirmed\\n   producing valid scene.edn; every logic.clj construct cross-checked\\n   against the proven vocabulary (defsystem/defn/def/let/when/cond/if/f32/\\n   spawn-entity/despawn-entity/set-position!/set-velocity!/get-x/get-y/\\n   nearest-tagged/count-tagged/doseq-entities/move-toward!/axis/tick-n/mod/\\n   rand-int/zero?/</=/not=/defatom/set-atom!) -- multiplication was checked\\n   and found NOT confirmed anywhere in any reference game or prior verified\\n   base, so score-reset-style periods use precomputed constants with a\\n   comment explaining the derivation, rather than an unverified `*` call.\\n   WASM compilation (kotoba-lang/engine's compile-file) was NOT attempted\\n   for all 18 in this pass -- a legitimate, valuable follow-up, out of\\n   scope here given this session's established slow-build cost for that\\n   verification tier\"}", :db/id -1, :adr/problem "{:source \"user request: pasted a PlayStation Store genre-browse page\\n            (real commercial titles as genre examples), asked for just the\\n            category labels extracted, then asked for a base gameplay\\n            system per category 'as kotoba-lang'\", :investigated [\"the pasted list mixed two things: real commercial game titles/key-art\\n    descriptions (copyrighted, must not be reproduced/mimicked) and generic\\n    genre category labels (not copyrightable, safe to use as organizing\\n    labels for wholly original systems) -- the category-label-only\\n    extraction the user explicitly requested resolved this cleanly before\\n    any system-building began\" \"the kami-clj compiler subset's proven vocabulary, established\\n    cumulatively across this whole session's isekai-network work, is\\n    genuinely constrained: single local input stream (no player-index axis\\n    argument anywhere), no terrain/tilemap/pathfinding/vehicle-physics/\\n    skeletal-animation reachable from guest logic (Rust-substrate only),\\n    no multiplication operator confirmed anywhere in any reference game or\\n    prior verified base system (checked before use, not assumed) -- every\\n    system in this repo was built to fit inside these real constraints,\\n    not an idealized unconstrained design\" \"4 of the 18 requested categories are not gameplay archetypes at all --\\n    forcing them into the same 'distinct core loop' shape as the other 14\\n    would have meant fabricating fictional mechanics with no grounding,\\n    the same failure mode this session's design-investigation work (concept\\n    2-5 open-questions) repeatedly rejected for isekai-network itself\"], :risk \"18 base systems is a large surface -- risk of quality dilution\\n         (shallow, near-identical copies) or risk of dishonest padding on\\n         the 4 non-mechanic categories to hit a round number. Both were\\n         explicitly guarded against: real per-genre design distinctions\\n         (see :decision below), and honest N/A/preset framing for the 4\\n         business/setting categories rather than fabricated mechanics\"}", :adr/alternatives "[{:option \"force all 18 categories into an identical 'distinct core loop'\\n  